### Verdict: NEI

### Justification
The claim that US lockdowns are "partly responsible" for non-COVID-19 excess deaths in 2020 is not directly supported nor refuted by the available evidence. The CDC confirmed that approximately one-third of excess deaths from January through October 2020 were not attributed to COVID-19, with leading causes including heart disease, Alzheimer disease/dementia, and diabetes ([CDC MMWR](https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/pdfs/mm6942e2-H.pdf)), and acknowledged these deaths may be "indirectly related to the pandemic (e.g., because of disruptions in health care access or utilization)" or misclassified COVID-19 deaths. However, no source reviewed isolates or quantifies lockdowns as a distinct causal factor separate from broader pandemic effects. Instead, non-COVID-19 excess deaths are attributed to general pandemic-related factors such as fear of seeking medical care ([Penn Today](https://penntoday.upenn.edu/news/US-covid-deaths-may-be-underestimated-36-percent)), economic and mental health impacts, and healthcare disruptions. Additionally, a [PLOS ONE study](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0261759) defined US lockdown measures but did not link them to non-COVID-19 excess mortality. The CDC has not explicitly attributed any portion of non-COVID-19 excess deaths to lockdown policies, and the claim's specific causal assertion lacks direct evidentiary support in the reviewed sources.